---
import Bench from "./Bench.astro";
import Pipeline from "./Pipeline.astro";
import { bounds, pack, story } from "../../lib/coverage";
import { dunmoreSplit, hartwell, pipeline, spell } from "../../lib/cases";

const signed = (n: number) => (n > 0 ? `+${n}` : String(n));
const dots = [
  { label: "schedule pages saved", of: pipeline.asked, got: pipeline.saved, lost: pipeline.silent },
  { label: "finished matches read", of: story.finals, got: story.counted, lost: bounds.unseen },
];
---

<section id="case-hartwell">
  <h2>Case 2. Hartwell: gaps arrive in blocks</h2>
  <Pipeline small lit="build" note="This case happens at Build" />

  <p class="cv-callout">Coach Dolan, Hartwell: “We went into Dunmore October fifteenth and put seven on 'em. Seven-nothing. You've got us fourth.” Hartwell's own schedule page loaded fine, yet its row is still wrong.</p>

  <h3 class="cv-beat"><span class="sr-only">Case 2, </span>Look</h3>
  <p>Kick read each match once, from the home team's page, so no match would be counted twice. That {hartwell.seven} win was played at Dunmore.</p>
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="rows">
    <div data-q data-type="number" data-answer={pipeline.rows} data-why={`All ${spell(pipeline.rows)}. Dunmore is missing its ${spell(bounds.unseen)} home matches, and each of the other ${spell(bounds.unseen)} schools is missing its away match at Dunmore.`}>
      <label for="cv-rows">Predict first: only one schedule page is missing. How many of the {spell(pipeline.rows)} rows in the standings table are wrong?</label>
      <input id="cv-rows" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" data-save="q-rows" />
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Submit your prediction to open the interactive simulator.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check and open simulator</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal">
      <p>Try toggling school sites offline and online below. Taking down a single page wipes out an entire row of matches, breaking standings for teams whose own pages loaded fine.</p>
      <Bench id="bench-pages" controls={["pages"]} caption="Friday as it happened. Click a school to toggle its site offline or online." />
      <p>Now leave Dunmore offline and change how Kick reads each match.</p>
      <Bench id="bench-rule" controls={["rule"]} show={["counts", "matrix", "table"]} caption="Dunmore's site is still down. Switch the reading rule." />
      <p>When Kick reads both schools' pages, all {spell(bounds.unseen)} missing matches are recovered from the visitors' pages, and Hartwell Tech moves up to second.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure class="cv-split">
    <table>
      <caption>How the missing block skews one team's stats</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Dunmore</th><th scope="col">Matches</th><th scope="col">Goals conceded / match</th><th scope="col">Goal difference</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">In Kick's table</th><td>{dunmoreSplit.kick.p}</td><td>{dunmoreSplit.kick.perMatch}</td><td>{signed(dunmoreSplit.kick.gd)}</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Across all {dunmoreSplit.all.p}</th><td>{dunmoreSplit.all.p}</td><td>{dunmoreSplit.all.perMatch}</td><td>{signed(dunmoreSplit.all.gd)}</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <figcaption>Kick's table included all of Dunmore's away matches and none of its home matches, making a defense that struggled at home look solid.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>That distortion is <strong>selection bias</strong>: which matches went missing wasn't random, so the remaining matches aren't a representative sample of the season. Statisticians group missing data into three patterns based on what caused the gap:</p>
  <ul class="cv-three">
    <li><b>Missing completely at random</b><span>Nothing about the item determined whether it went missing, like a coin toss. The data you have is a fair sample, just smaller.</span></li>
    <li><b>Missing at random, given something you observe</b><span>A recorded variable explains the gap. Here, it's venue: every missing match was a Dunmore home game. Stats unrelated to venue stay fair; stats that depend on venue get skewed.</span></li>
    <li><b>Missing not at random</b><span>The missing values themselves caused the gap, like a school pulling its page down after a losing streak. The coverage record alone can't prove or rule this out.</span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="bias">
    <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer="mar" data-why="Missing at random, given the venue. Whether a match was lost depends on who hosted it, which we can see, and not on the score." data-wrong={JSON.stringify({ mcar: "A random coin toss would drop home and away games across different schools. Here, every lost game was a Dunmore home match.", mnar: "That would mean the outage happened because of the scores. Nothing in the record suggests that." })}>
      <label><span>Which missingness pattern describes the seven lost matches?</span>
        <select data-save="bias-kind"><option value="">Choose</option><option value="mcar">Missing completely at random</option><option value="mar">Missing at random, given the venue</option><option value="mnar">Missing not at random</option></select>
      </label>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer="venue" data-why="Any Dunmore stat where home and away performance differ. Games played is simply short, while goals conceded per match gets skewed because only away games were kept." data-wrong={JSON.stringify({ none: "Each individual match was parsed accurately, but the subset of matches is skewed.", points: "Points are short (and bounds handle that), while selection bias skews any rate or average where home and away performance differ." })}>
      <label><span>Which of Dunmore's stats in Kick's table does this missing block skew?</span>
        <select data-save="bias-which"><option value="">Choose</option><option value="none">None, since every match included was read correctly</option><option value="points">Only its points</option><option value="venue">Any stat where home and away performance differ</option></select>
      </label>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer="low" data-why={`Too low. Dunmore conceded more goals at home, and every home match was missing, so ${dunmoreSplit.kick.perMatch} jumped to ${dunmoreSplit.all.perMatch} once all ${dunmoreSplit.all.p} matches were included.`}>
      <label><span>Which way did it skew Dunmore's goals conceded per match?</span>
        <select data-save="bias-way"><option value="">Choose</option><option value="low">Too low</option><option value="high">Too high</option><option value="none">Not at all</option></select>
      </label>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Answer all three, then check.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check answers</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal"><p>Before the home results were recovered, you couldn't tell which direction the stat was skewed. A coverage record can identify the cause of a gap and which numbers it affects, but not which way they lean.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="cv-beat"><span class="sr-only">Case 2, </span>Name it</h3>
  <p class="cv-lede">Gaps arrive in blocks. A single outage wipes out related records together and hits the same teams repeatedly.</p>
  <p>That's why you count in the <strong>unit of analysis</strong>, the unit your claim is actually about. Counted in pages, Kick lost 1 of {spell(pipeline.asked)}. But a standings table makes claims about matches, and in matches that single page cost {bounds.unseen} of {story.finals} and threw off every row.</p>
  <div class="cv-dots">
    {dots.map((d) => (
      <>
        <p><b>{d.got} of {d.of}</b> {d.label}</p>
        <div role="img" aria-label={`${d.got} of ${d.of} ${d.label}, ${d.lost} lost with Dunmore's page${d.of - d.got - d.lost ? `, ${d.of - d.got - d.lost} with no score` : ""}`}>{Array.from({ length: d.of }, (_, i) => <i class={i < d.got ? "is-read" : i < d.got + d.lost ? "is-lost" : "is-noscore"}></i>)}</div>
      </>
    ))}
    <ul class="cv-key">
      <li><i class="is-read"></i>Arrived and read</li>
      <li><i class="is-lost"></i>Lost with Dunmore's page</li>
      <li><i class="is-noscore"></i>Final, no score</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <p>Even before a missing result is recovered, you can still limit its uncertainty. Kick's table puts Hartwell Tech at {hartwell.pts} points with one match unread. A loss adds 0 and a win adds 3, so Hartwell's true total must fall between {hartwell.pts} and {hartwell.high}. That range is a <strong>bound</strong>: you don't know the exact value, but you know the limits it cannot cross.</p>

  <h3 class="cv-beat"><span class="sr-only">Case 2, </span>What Touchline does</h3>
  <p>Touchline reads both schools' schedule pages and deduplicates each match, so a match is only lost if <em>both</em> sites are unavailable. The trade-off is an extra matching step: the same match, written differently on two sites, has to be recognized as one game. <a href="../05-identity/">Module 5</a> covers how that works.</p>

  <h3 class="cv-beat"><span class="sr-only">Case 2, </span>Your claim</h3>
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="claim-bounds">
    <div data-q data-type="range" data-answer={`${bounds.points},${bounds.high}`} data-why={`${bounds.points} to ${bounds.high}. If Dunmore lost all ${spell(bounds.unseen)}, it stays at ${bounds.points}. If it won all ${spell(bounds.unseen)}, it gains ${3 * bounds.unseen} points. Its actual total turned out to be ${pack.dunmorePoints}, right inside the bound.`}>
      <p class="cv-qlabel">Kick's table gives Dunmore {bounds.points} points from {spell(bounds.played)} matches, with {spell(bounds.unseen)} matches unread. Fill in the bound that belongs beside it.</p>
      <p class="cv-row cv-fillin">
        <span>Dunmore: {bounds.points} points so far, truly between</span>
        <input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Lowest possible total" data-save="q-low" />
        <span>and</span>
        <input inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Highest possible total" data-save="q-high" />
      </p>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">Enter both endpoints, then check.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check bounds</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal"><p>Bounds tell readers exactly who is affected and how far each number could move, before a single missing score is recovered.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p class="cv-next">Next: Case 3. Even with every schedule page answering, one match is still missing a score.</p>
</section>
